sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design, container runtime (ADR 0250 step 3, docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-default-instance): the automatic development deployment of docs/reference/deployments.md, in the default Instance's container. Each new commit of the default branch deploys the container default once: the Gateway checks the branch head before it starts development policy; Swamp keeps the retry and admission state. The default of a nested-fleet Project never takes this path: it deploys after each Gateway release, with its inner fleet: container/fleet-refresh.mmd. When the charts move into place, this becomes the development branch of the deploy chart.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · automatic deployment policy
  %% kind: instance.deploy
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane C icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>policy · execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant C as Container<br/>default Instance
  loop every minute
    Note over GW: Check eligible defaults<br/>recorded placement and seed
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - selects only active container defaults with active app-dev placement whose Project has default_auto_deploy on and no nested fleet, using the automatic admission eligibility rule
    %%> - skips a default whose last policy result is commit_failed for this head, and waits 10 minutes after a failed one, so a failed commit does not loop; a manual deploy after that result lifts the hold
    %%> - reads each eligible Project's default branch once through the cached source reader; no environment-template refresh
    alt seed matches branch head
      Note over GW: Nothing to deploy<br/>no Swamp run
    else default is behind
      GW->>SW: Run development policy<br/>only the selected Instance IDs
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - shares the retained behavior identity and development state; keeps pending admissions, backoff and retry limits
      %%> - never discovers or admits production work; the production schedule and its pause stay unchanged
      %%> - one deploy at a time: an open Operation on the default reports deploying or busy; the next run takes the newest head
  SW->>GW: Admit the deployment<br/>instance:deploy-automatic
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Gateway admits `instance.deploy` for that exact commit, with the Instance lock and a repository read token in the vault
  Note over GW: Capture source facts<br/>before the write lock
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads before the write transaction; admission rechecks eligibility, source configuration and idempotency before recording facts; see /reference/deployments#container-source-facts
  %%> - refuses admission when capture or source classification fails
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>instance-deploy-direct
  %%> - the long steps (prepare, checkout, each dev deploy step, finish, the fleet restore) lock a Swamp behavior of this Instance on its Node, so deployments of two defaults on one Node never wait for each other; the Instance lock keeps two deployments of one default apart
  SW->>N: Start the container<br/>Ansible · start · probe
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one play runs the phases `start` and `probe`, in this order, before the seed hold; the Project's PHP waits for `php` in "Prepare the container"
  %%> - `probe`: fetches the branch into the Node's mirror and refuses a pinned commit off its first-parent chain, as `fetch` does; then reads the workspace and decides a checkout already past the commit (docs/cli/instance/deploy.mmd)
  %%> - `start`: starts a stopped (asleep) default container; the start waits only for the units the container's boot starts: FrankenPHP and the enabled Process units; a new or newly running Process is the work of `processes`
  %%> - an older container's app account moves to the stock `www-data` (uid and gid 33) as soon as it runs, before any other command in it, by root's one swap file `/usr/local/libexec/orbit-app-user`; a moved container only reads, and a nested-fleet container keeps `orbit` and `work` for its inner fleet, with `www-data` in `work`; its older managed firewall's global reset becomes a reset of Orbit's table before the new guard is loaded, preserving the inner Incus tables
  %%> - `start` of a sleeping container (frozen or stopped by a sleep): resumes it through the power helper's `restore`, which reads the plan's pages back while frozen, consumes the plan and closes swap on each cgroup of `limits.json` that still exists, or discards the plan and boots a stopped one, then removes the asleep marker once the `start` part passed (the container runs and its units are active; no web probe), so the Route serves the app without a wake; the result records `power_state` `running`; the end of the deployment counts as activity, `deployed_recently`
  %%> - `start`, before the boot of a stopped container: the presented machine name: a stopped container gets `/etc/hostname`, `/etc/machine-info` and the `127.0.1.1` line of `/etc/hosts` through the file API before the boot, so the boot gives the kernel and T3 the name; a running or frozen container gets nothing, and nothing restarts
  %%> - `start` of a nested-fleet container also delivers the Club key: pipelined module stdin into the container's /run (a tmpfs, never a snapshot), before any check of the inner fleet; the result's `nested_fleet_club_key` is what this write did (`written`, `written_serve_not_restarted`, `unchanged`, `refused`); without a vault copy it says `missing`
  SW->>N: Prepare the container<br/>Ansible · php · fetch
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one play runs the phases `php` and `fetch`, in this order, after the seed hold; a nested-fleet default runs its fleet check and first seed between them: container/fleet-refresh.mmd
  %%> - `php`: the Project's PHP link and settings, and a restart of the running units when the settings changed
  %%> - `fetch`: fetches into the Node's mirror and pushes the branch into the container as a bundle; the token never enters the container
  %%> - the bundle holds only what the workspace lacks: its prerequisite is the newest commit the branch shares with the workspace's HEAD, or the tip's parent when the workspace already holds the tip; a workspace whose HEAD the mirror does not hold (local commits) or that has none (a first deploy) gets the whole branch; the checkout sees the same refs as with the whole branch, so its refusals stay as they are
  %%> - a pinned commit off the branch's first-parent chain refuses `deployment.commit_not_on_branch`
  %%> Why one step:
  %%> - neither phase changes the workspace, so they share their failure handling: nothing is restored
  %%> - one play instead of two saves one Ansible start, about 2.5 s
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - the code names the failed phase, such as `instance.container_start_failed` or `instance.container_fetch_failed`; a failed `start` retains its task name and bounded stderr in the public detail, except for a private task; no snapshot is restored
  %%> - a sleeping container's `start`: `instance.power_helper_busy` when another power action on the Instance held the power helper's lock for 10 s, `instance.power_helper_failed` when the helper refused its input or crashed; either leaves the asleep marker and the container's power state as they were
  SW->>C: Check out in the container<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same checks as a checkout default: a dirty tree refuses `deployment.checkout_dirty`, a diverged branch `deployment.branch_diverged`
  %%> - a checkout already past the commit never reaches it: the `probe` of "Start the container" decides that case first (docs/cli/instance/deploy.mmd)
  %%> - Orbit's Git calls, the repack, the `.env` exclude line and the deploy steps' record run as `agent` (ADR 0268)
  %%> - after the checkout, the default's untracked files that Git does not ignore go, as the workspace's owner with the task check's Git settings, in a task copy's clean rounds; `.env` is excluded first and stays, ignored and tracked files stay; a file it cannot remove does not fail the deployment, but a clean that fails or runs past 60 s does; the result's `workspace_cleaned` names what went, names only
  %%> - after the checkout, a bounded repack in the foreground (`git repack -d --geometric=2`, 30 s): each fetch adds a pack and Orbit's Git calls start no automatic maintenance; it first removes the temporary files of an earlier repack that are an hour old; a repack that fails or times out prints one `orbit-workspace-repack=` line and never fails the deployment
  %%> - after the repack and before the steps, root's workspace pass gives the group back what the owner has and gives `agent` each entry that `www-data` owns; it never follows a link and prints only a count above 20 entries; phase `processes` runs it again after the steps
  Note over SW: Read the steps to skip<br/>run folder
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - reads the names the checkout found unchanged; only a deployment with a step that has `inputs` runs it
  SW->>C: Run dev deploy steps<br/>incus exec · vault copy
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Project's development deploy steps, as `agent`, the workspace's owner, with `HOME=/home/agent` (ADR 0268): they install changed dependencies and migrate
  %%> - a best-effort step (`required: false`) discards its output and prints only its command's exit code; the Gateway records that code in the result's `best_effort_steps` (an integer 0-255, else null, never the text), and a nonzero code does not fail the deployment
  %%> - a step with `inputs` runs only when they or its command changed since its last passed run in this workspace: the checkout compares the Git object IDs of its inputs at the commit and its command's digest with the step's record in `.git/orbit-deploy-steps`, and the step's guard skips it when they match; a passed step records them; a step without `inputs` always runs
  %%> Why:
  %%> - most releases change no lock file: an install that finds nothing to do still costs its tool's start, its autoload and its scripts, about 4 s a step
  opt the proof Project's default
    SW->>GW: Hold the seed<br/>seed-hold call
    %%> When:
    %%> - before "Prepare the container", whose play runs the first `seed` phase of a nested-fleet default; only the proof Project's default, whose seed a seed copy sends (instance/seed-copy.mmd); its container intent's `seed_hold` names it; every other default skips the step
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks the Gateway for the default's seed hold `instance_seed:DEFAULT_ID` (`POST direct-operations/ID/seed-hold`); while a seed copy holds it (its Operation lock), the Gateway answers `423 instance.seed_busy` after 5 s and the step asks again, for up to 1,800 s (`ProofSeeds::HoldWaitSeconds`), which the run budget adds
    %%> - from its first busy answer the deployment waits for the seed, and once granted it holds the seed until it ends; while it waits or holds, a seed copy is not admitted (`instance.seed_held`), so the end of a running copy admits no next copy and the deployment goes first; the deployment's end, passed or failed, admits the copy again
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a retake deletes or renames `seed-COMMIT`, also at the same commit; a push of that snapshot to a CI Node at that moment fails the seed copy, and the retake's own snapshot calls can fail too (docs/reference/development-containers.md#the-seed-hold)
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the bound passes while the seed copy runs: the deployment fails with `instance.seed_hold_timeout` before it changes the container; the old seed stays; no seed is restored and the container is not started
  end
  SW->>C: Finish the container<br/>Ansible · processes · seed · check
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one play runs the phases `processes`, `php_index`, `seed` and `check`, in this order; a nested-fleet default refreshes its fleet between `processes` and `php_index`: container/fleet-refresh.mmd
  %%> - the handlers a phase notifies run before the next phase starts, as with separate plays
  %%> Phase processes:
  %%> - installs the Project's new and changed development Process definitions as `orbit-*` units, with their environment files from the vault
  %%> - restarts a changed unit and removes a former environment file
  %%> - removes the unit and environment folder of a Process whose definition the Project removed; admission marked its record `removing`
  %%> - enables and starts each running unit and waits for it; a unit that does not become active fails with its name in the detail, and only its Process is recorded failed
  %%> Phase php_index:
  %%> - only a Laravel default on an active app-dev Node, whose intent has `php_index`; every other default skips it
  %%> - `agent` runs Phpactor's `index:build` at the application's folder, after the deploy steps, at the lowest CPU and I/O priority and within 600 s; Phpactor finds its index under agent's home by itself, and reindexes only what changed
  %%> - an unchanged tree (its fingerprint in `~/.cache/orbit-php-index.json`) starts no Phpactor
  %%> - a failed or late build fails with `instance.php_index_failed` and Phpactor's reason in the detail; the seed is not taken, so the old seed stays
  %%> - a Node whose runtime folder predates Phpactor (neither its `phpactor` part nor `bin/phpactor`: not converged since the release) skips the work: the deployment passes, its seed has no new index, and its result has `php_index_skipped`
  %%> - a new fingerprint stays pending until the seed phase takes the seed that holds the index, so a failed snapshot leaves the next run to build and retake again
  %%> Phase seed:
  %%> - prepares a Node/Vite default’s private Node store as www-data before the existing snapshot stop/start boundary; this conversion itself never restarts a Process
  %%> - a converted store requires a new seed even at the same commit, so clones inherit it; older seeds remain slower but correct until the explicit deploy refreshes them
  %%> - stops the default's Processes for a moment, snapshots the container and starts them again, so clones copy no half-written file
  %%> - right before the snapshot, the files every clone needs go into the default (the container intent's `seed_files`), and `/etc/orbit/seed-manifest.json` records what the seed holds, its Orbit units that no clone keeps, and whether its tree is clean; a clone's prepare reads it once and writes only what differs (development-containers.md#the-seed-manifest)
  %%> - every passed deployment retakes `seed-<commit>`, also at the same commit, so a clone copies the default's current caches and built files
  %%> - deletes older seed snapshots that no clone uses
  %%> - the proof default keeps its previous seed as `base-<commit>` when a CI Node that is ready for the seed copy records that seed, so the next seed copy to that Node sends only the change; the Gateway names that seed in the intent (`seed_base`, from `ProofSeeds`); every older `base-` pair goes; a rename needs no free ID range (development-containers.md#a-retake-keeps-the-seed)
  %%> - a default with an inner volume: the volume's snapshot of the same name is the other half of the seed; it is taken right after the container's, retaken when it is missing, and the older halves go only after both new halves exist. Right before the volume's half, `sync -f /srv/inner-pool` as root in the container commits btrfs, so the snapshot holds what the fleet wrote; a failed flush or volume snapshot deletes the new halves and fails with `instance.seed_volume_flush_failed` or `instance.seed_volume_snapshot_failed`; the old pair stays
  %%> - while the deployment is open, a clone of the default refuses `instance.default_seed_in_flight`, so no copy reads one half before the retake and the other after it
  %%> - starting the units again restarts every running Process on the new commit, such as a queue worker
  %%> Phase check:
  %%> - the branch is checked out at the commit and the units are active, else `deployment.branch_changed` or `deployment.processes_inactive`
  %%> Why one step:
  %%> - all three run after the checkout and share their failure handling; one play instead of three saves two Ansible starts, about 5 s
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - the code names the failed phase, such as `instance.container_processes_failed`, or is the phase's own refusal
  alt every required step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    Note over GW: Record the seed commit
    %%> Result:
    %%> - deletes the Processes marked `removing` and names them in `processes_removed`
    %%> - new clones start at this snapshot; existing clones do not change
  else a required step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - a step that waited 60 s for a behavior lock and never ran records `instance.deploy_lock_timeout`, not the step's own code
    %%> - no new seed snapshot is taken, so clones keep starting at the last commit that deployed; Swamp's policy retries after its backoff
    %%> - the history row records the admitted commit, so Swamp's policy counts the failure for that commit
  end
  end
  end
